      "label": "fig:invariant-versus-certificate",
      "file": "Figure_06.pdf",
      "sha256": "3673265b5d194588235c0e045e2ab74947c0ea2d9800cf2485ebe5585905b927",
      "bytes": 115221,
      "pages": 1,
      "title_tex": "Renumbering invariance and exact identity have different obligations.",
      "legend_tex": "The first two colored graphs are isomorphic under the displayed renumbering. The third has two connected components and is not isomorphic to them. All three have six identically colored vertices, degree two at each vertex, and identical edge colors, so the displayed vertex-count/degree invariant collides. A full canonical encoding identifies the renumbered pair and separates the nonisomorphic graph. Numbers are display identifiers, not vertex colors. The example concerns colored-graph identity rather than stereochemical perception. The independent assignment-partition checks require both invariance within reference classes and separation across them.",
      "title_words": 8,
      "conservative_legend_words": 88,
      "all_fonts_embedded": true,
      "caption_removed_from_graphic": true,
      "crop_margin_points": 1,
