    {
      "figure_number": 7,
      "label": "fig:mirror-canonical-examples",
      "file": "Figure_07.pdf",
      "sha256": "8d1417b53d4337b438cc4f4918d7aebef6fadafca4b170044bfaec131584d4e8",
      "bytes": 118086,
      "pages": 1,
      "title_tex": "Whole-molecule mirror canonicalization in six real chemical cases.",
      "legend_tex": "Each panel header gives the support and the improper index \\(\\left[\\widehat\\Gamma:\\Gamma\\right]\\) of Definition~\\ref{def:mirror-local}, which decides whether \\(\\mu\\) can move the local state at all; the verdict counts distinct certificates by Corollary~\\ref{cor:mirror}. Fischer projections place horizontal bonds toward the viewer and vertical bonds away. (A) \\(R/S\\)-bromochlorofluoromethane gives two certificates. (B) For \\emph{meso}-2,3-dibromobutane the index is two at both centers, yet the center-exchange automorphism \\(\\sigma\\) identifies the mirror and one certificate remains: compensation is a property of the global quotient, not of the local index. (C) The planar bond has index one, so spatial reflection preserves the \\(E\\) state of but-2-ene and does not manufacture \\(Z\\). (D) \\(P/M\\)-penta-2,3-diene, (E) \\(P/M\\)-[6]helicene, and (F) \\(pR/pS\\)-1-bromo-2-methylferrocene each retain two mirror-related certificates, the last two through the declared sense inversion of a path and of an oriented plane. One global comparison therefore covers atom, bond, axis, path, and plane carriers as well as meso compensation. Arbitrary atom renumberings within each stereograph have already been quotiented by canonicalization.",
      "title_words": 8,
      "conservative_legend_words": 180,
      "all_fonts_embedded": true,
      "caption_removed_from_graphic": true,
      "crop_margin_points": 1,
